60,3,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I think... Candidate C, the Supplier Data Hub, would stop the misrouted deliveries and the copying of records.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,May,,"@Daisuke, wait. Who manages that hub? If it's the coordination group, they'll quit. Candidate D, the Workflow Assistant, actually helps them!","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"@Daisuke, Candidate A is a disaster. I've seen the network logs. A ""mobile app"" for sites with no signal is just a fancy way to lose data.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,Candidate A and B likely require high capital for hardware or studio setup. Candidate C and D are software-based.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"@May, Candidate D sounds like one of those ""everyone must use it"" tools. My research says those are the ones that get abandoned!","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Daisuke,,"But... Candidate C solves a real problem. The delivery errors are happening now. It's not just a ""nice to have.""","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,May,,"But @Daisuke, look at the developer availability! Candidate C needs a huge database migration. Candidate D is a tool that reduces the burden on staff.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, Candidate D is a luxury. Candidate C is a necessity, provided it's a fixed-fee vendor solution and not a custom build.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"I am concerned about the licensing for Candidate D. If every employee gets a license, the renewal cost next year will be astronomical.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Kenji,,See! Candidate C is a central hub. It's a specific tool for a specific purpose. That's the pattern for survival!,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Daisuke,,I... I feel like we should prioritize the people who are making mistakes because of the system. Candidate C helps them.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,May,,"I can't support C. It's just more data for the coordination group to ""clean."" Candidate D is the only one that gives time back to the employees.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, your empathy is touching, but irrational. Candidate D is a productivity toy. Candidate C fixes a broken operational chain.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,Candidate C is the most reasonable. It addresses a specific inefficiency and can be managed as a fixed-fee service without scaling costs.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"I agree with Ryuji and Shunichi. Candidate C is a targeted fix. It's not trying to change the whole company's culture, just the data.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Daisuke,,I'll vote for C. It's the only one that addresses the daily losses I found.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,May,,I'm sticking with D. We can't keep treating staff time as if it's free. We need to automate the drudgery.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,I'll vote C. It's the only one that doesn't rely on a miracle of network coverage or an infinite budget.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,I vote C. It aligns with the available software allowance and avoids the risk of scaling license fees.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Kenji,,I'm voting C too! It's a practical tool that actually has a chance of surviving its first year.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
